Subject: [PATCH 5/5] prevent a second submit of an already-sent auth form

A login form submitted TWICE orphans the OAuth2 login challenge and delivers
the operator to Ory's stock welcome page instead of the application they asked
for. Retrying works, which is what makes it read as intermittent flakiness
rather than a defect.

MEASURED, not inferred. From the Kratos log on mgmt-zagros-01-thor for the
2026-08-16 production incident — note that both POSTs carry the SAME referer,
the SAME flow id and the SAME content-length:

  08:31:22.996  POST /self-service/login  len=195
                referer .../login?flow=1ca1adb6-500b-4237-affd-8407486c4506
  08:31:23.652  -> 303, "Identity authenticated successfully"   (aal1 session)
  08:31:23.693  -> browser follows to login/browser WITH the challenge (correct)
  08:31:23.806  POST /self-service/login  len=195   <-- SAME form, 810ms later
                referer .../login?flow=1ca1adb6-500b-4237-affd-8407486c4506
  08:31:23.812  -> 303 https://auth.zagros.netbird.zagros.example/
  08:31:23.891  /welcome -> requireAuth -> /sessions/whoami -> 403
  08:31:23.923  -> bare ?aal=aal2, no login_challenge. Challenge orphaned.

No `PUT /admin/oauth2/auth/requests/login/accept` was ever issued for that
challenge. One of two real login challenges in a five-hour window was lost.

REPRODUCED DETERMINISTICALLY 2026-08-17 against the live goatnet by rendering
the form once, keeping its action and csrf_token, and POSTing twice — exactly
what a browser sends on a double-click, because it never re-fetches the flow:

  submit #1 -> 303 .../self-service/login/browser?aal=aal2&login_challenge=…
  submit #2 -> 303 https://auth.zagros.netbird.zagros.example/

WHAT THIS PATCH IS NOT. For several hours the recorded fix for this was "teach
maybeInitiate2FA to carry the login_challenge". That is not implementable: by
the time that middleware runs, the browser is on /welcome, req.url is
/welcome, and no challenge is in scope to copy. maybeInitiate2FA is the LAST
link in the chain, not the first. The first link is the duplicate submission,
and that is what this patch removes.

SERIALIZATION SAFETY. Kratos's login form carries `method=password` as the
submit BUTTON's own name/value pair. A button disabled synchronously inside a
submit handler is excluded from form serialization, so the POST would go out
without `method` and every login would fail validation. The guard therefore
blocks the second submit EVENT and defers any visual disabling to a macrotask,
after the browser has serialized the body. `verify-patched.sh` asserts the
deferral, not merely the marker, because a correct-looking synchronous version
breaks all logins.

ONCE PER DOCUMENT. The partial this lands in is included twice on the consent
page: consent.hbs includes it and so does layouts/auth.hbs, which consent.hbs
renders inside. Registered twice, the guard cancelled the FIRST submit — the
first copy marked the form, the second copy saw the mark on the same event and
prevented it — so "Allow" sent nothing. Measured on zagros 2026-09-17 with the
only client there that shows the consent page (every other one is first-party
with skip_consent): GET /consent 200 twice for one challenge, no POST /consent
at Traefik, no consent/accept at Hydra. The guard now installs once per
document, and tests/double-submit-guard.test.js runs it included once AND twice.

Applied to a VIEW rather than to lib/routes/consent.js, so it ships via
apply-view-patch.sh instead of apply-patch.sh.

--- a/views/partials/scripts.hbs
+++ b/views/partials/scripts.hbs
@@ -1,2 +1,49 @@
 {{! Scripts }}
 <script src="assets/passwordInput.js"></script>
+
+{{! goat-maintainer-double-submit-guard }}
+<script>
+  (function () {
+    // Install ONCE per document. This partial is not included once per page:
+    // consent.hbs includes it AND renders inside layouts/auth.hbs, which
+    // includes it again. Two copies of the listener below both see the same
+    // submit event — the first marks the form, the second finds the mark and
+    // cancels the FIRST submission. "Allow" then sends nothing at all.
+    if (window.__dlfSubmitGuardInstalled) return;
+    window.__dlfSubmitGuardInstalled = true;
+
+    // One submission per rendered form. A second submit of the same flow is
+    // answered by Kratos on a consumed flow and navigates the browser off an
+    // in-flight OAuth2 login, orphaning the Hydra challenge.
+    document.addEventListener(
+      "submit",
+      function (event) {
+        var form = event.target;
+        if (!form || form.tagName !== "FORM") return;
+
+        if (form.hasAttribute("data-dlf-submitted")) {
+          // Already sent. Swallow this one; the first response is still coming.
+          event.preventDefault();
+          event.stopImmediatePropagation();
+          return;
+        }
+        form.setAttribute("data-dlf-submitted", "true");
+
+        // Defer the visual disable to a macrotask. Disabling a submit button
+        // synchronously drops its name/value (method=password) from the POST
+        // body — the form would serialize without the method Kratos requires.
+        window.setTimeout(function () {
+          var controls = form.querySelectorAll(
+            'button[type="submit"], input[type="submit"]'
+          );
+          for (var i = 0; i < controls.length; i++) {
+            controls[i].setAttribute("aria-disabled", "true");
+            controls[i].style.pointerEvents = "none";
+            controls[i].style.opacity = "0.6";
+          }
+        }, 0);
+      },
+      true // capture: run before any framework handler can re-dispatch
+    );
+  })();
+</script>
